WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.900
Testing LLM outputs directly.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.876
Free text has no right answer to compare against.

3
00:00:05.876 --> 00:00:07.733
So how do you write a test for it?

4
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:12.800
There are exactly three ways, and each trades cost for flexibility.

5
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.156
At the bottom, structured checks.

6
00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:19.267
If the output is constrained, assert on the parsed field directly.

7
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:20.700
Cheapest and strictest.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.700
Next, semantic similarity.

9
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.800
Embed the output and a reference, and demand a cosine score above a threshold.

10
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.834
At the top, an LLM judge.

11
00:00:29.834 --> 00:00:33.786
A second model grades the first against a written rubric.

12
00:00:33.786 --> 00:00:35.867
Most flexible, most expensive.

13
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:39.700
Watch out for one blind spot in similarity.

14
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.660
It checks meaning, not truth, so a nicely worded wrong answer still passes.

15
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.400
Add a judge for correctness.

16
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.133
Here is how a judge works.

17
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:54.433
We give it a written rubric, such as names the root cause, no invented timestamps.

18
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:57.433
Then we show it the source and the candidate answer.

19
00:00:57.733 --> 00:00:59.924
It returns a verdict with a reason.

20
00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:04.367
Calibrate it on human labeled examples, and run it at temperature zero.

21
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:07.333
Here are all three in one test.

22
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:11.133
First, the severity field must be one of the allowed values.

23
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:15.667
Second, the text must be semantically close to an analyst written reference.

24
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:20.067
Third, the judge applies a rubric, and we print its reason on failure.

25
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.467
A real case from Mission Control RAG.

26
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:28.333
A model upgrade rephrases the CME arrival, but the time is the same.

27
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:33.433
The exact match suite goes red on forty cases, and eats an afternoon of triage.

28
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:39.067
The semantic plus judge suite passes all forty, because the actual answer never changed.

29
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:41.104
Let's recap.

30
00:01:41.104 --> 00:01:43.967
Three styles: exact, semantic, and judge.

31
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.233
The costlier ones are more flexible.

32
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:48.200
Similarity can't check facts.

33
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.532
And always calibrate your judge.

34
00:01:50.532 --> 00:01:53.200
Next up: adversarial and red-team testing.
